# Tutorial 26 — JES Worlds, Physics, RPG and AI
# Reference: docs/guides/jes-by-example/07-tilemap-world.md
# Read scripts/tutorial/README.md for the complete course and linked references.
@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_26
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: JVN also has systems for interactive 2D worlds. This workshop maps those systems and gives you a practical build sequence; the linked guides contain complete runnable projects.
@label lesson_menu
> Tilemaps and movement -> maps
> Physics and simulation -> physics
> RPG state and AI -> rpg
> Build a tiny exploration scene -> practice
> Checkpoint -> checkpoint
> Finish and return to the hub -> return_hub
@label maps
Lavender: A tilemap separates reusable tiles from their placement in layers. Collision data says where movement is blocked; it is not implied by a tile looking like a wall.
Lavender: Use the Tilemap Editor to lay out a small room. Add a controllable actor, collision, and a camera before expanding the map.
Lavender: Exercise: test each doorway and boundary, including diagonal movement. Then change the window size and check that the camera and input still agree.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label physics
Lavender: Physics bodies and shapes describe simulation. Visual sprites describe appearance. Align them deliberately so a collision feels like it matches the drawing.
Lavender: Fixed timestep and maximum substeps trade simulation consistency against work per frame. Test slow frames instead of assuming your development machine represents every player.
Lavender: The physics guide introduces bodies, forces, collision behavior, and tuning. Start with one falling object and a floor; add interactions after that is predictable.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label rpg
Lavender: RPG systems organize actors, stats, inventory, quests, and combat. Their state may need to cross the VNS/JES boundary and survive saving.
Lavender: AI systems drive behavior such as patrol, pursuit, and action selection. These are game behavior systems, separate from image generation or language-model dialogue.
Lavender: Use explicit state changes and observable goals. A patrol that can be inspected is easier to fix than an enemy that seems to change its mind without a reason.
Lavender: Exercise: write down which state belongs to an entity, which belongs to the party or quest, and which must be handed back to VNS.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label practice
Lavender: Build one room, one actor, one collectible, and one exit. Return to VNS with a collected-item result and branch the next conversation.
Lavender: Then save, reload, and verify that the item cannot be collected twice accidentally. Test keyboard input, collision boundaries, and returning to the paused dialogue.
Lavender: Follow the linked Tilemap World, RPG Systems, Physics Bodies, and VNS Bridge examples in order. This is an authoring exercise; no invisible RPG simulation is running behind this dialogue.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label checkpoint
Lavender: What makes a painted wall block the player?
> Collision data and movement or physics rules -> correct
> Its appearance alone -> retry
@label retry
Lavender: Rendering does not automatically establish collision. Configure the interaction explicitly.
[jump checkpoint]
@label correct
Lavender: Exactly. Verify both the visible world and the rules that govern it.
[jump return_hub]

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_26]
[flag tutorial_done_26]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_5]
